<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="b168ce73">&lt;p&gt;
    Release Plans:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Can vary in size and duration
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Are subject to change as the items contained in it evolve throughout the life of a project as new knowledge becomes
        available or changes to the scope of work occur, thereby impacting previously made assumptions
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Should be revisited and updated on a regular basis, in Agile this would ideally occur after each sprint
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        With frequent releases shorten the feedback cycle which ultimately reduces the risk of building a product that does
        not meet stakeholders&apos; expectations
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        May also include information on stakeholder demands, technology constraints and dependencies between work items
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Do not need to include release dates when employing either a continuous or flexible delivery approach
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="1a92a9f8"></attribute>
<attribute name="usageGuidance" nodeId="bef9e1c8"></attribute>
<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="fcff6701">&lt;p&gt;
    A Release Plan is an evolving flowchart that describes what features will be delivered in upcoming releases. The
    Release Plan contains:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        An ordered and estimated list of work to be done 
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Velocity estimates of the team and expected duration the team will be committed to working on the product
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Definition of the Minimum Viable Product(s) (MVP)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Conditions of satisfaction (e.g. goals for schedule, scope and resources)
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The Release Plan should clearly convey:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        When the team will release
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        What components of the product will be released
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        To whom the released components will be delivered
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    There are three typical approaches that the Release Plan can take depending on the release strategy being employed:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Continuous delivery&lt;/strong&gt; - the Release Plan will specify that developed components be
        released immediately once completed and signed off
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Phased delivery -&lt;/strong&gt; the Release Plan will specify that developed components be released at
        pre-determined dates which the team will work toward
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Flexible delivery -&lt;/strong&gt; the Release Plan will specify developed components be released once
        they are deemed sufficient by the team lead
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
</Element>
